Q: get-childitem and folder structures with folder names containing &#39;&amp;&#39; A: <p>You can do your own recursion, returning the objects in the order you require:</p>
<pre><code>Function Get-ChildDirRecurse {
[CmdletBinding()]
Param([Parameter(ValueFromPipeline=$true)]$Path)
    Process {
        $Path | Get-ChildItem -Directory | ForEach-Object {
            $_
            $_ | Get-ChildDirRecurse
        }
    }
}

$tree = Get-ChildDirRecurse -Path &quot;/Music&quot; | Select-Object -ExpandProperty FullName
$tree
</code></pre>

Q: Best way to handle similar type of function without writing them again in C++ A: <p>You could let the caller worry about it. Instead of a function pointer, your function could accept a function object, which shifts the burden of tracking extra parameters to the caller. So your &quot;big function&quot; could look like</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;functional&gt; // Header for `std::function`

double BigFunction(std::function&lt;double (double)&gt; func, double var)
//                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^-- Instead of a function pointer
{
    double var2 = var; // Placeholder: calculations that only use &quot;var&quot; variable
    double var3 = func(var2);
    return var3;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This would not impact call sites that pass a pointer to a function that takes just one parameter, as in your first case. (A <code>std::function</code> can be constructed from a suitable function pointer.) The call sites that need to pass extra parameters would be altered to pass a lambda instead of a function pointer.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>// Demo function with an extra parameter
double foo(double, double*) { return 2.7; }

int main() {
    double var = 9.8;
    double * para = nullptr; // Demo parameter

    return BigFunction([para](double var2) { return foo(var2, para); }, var);
    //                                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^-- wrapped call
    //                        ^^^^^^^^^^^-- To be supplied by BigFunction
    //                 ^^^^^^-- The extra parameters are captured
}
</code></pre>
<p>A benefit of this approach is that there is only one copy of <code>BigFunction</code> in the executable, whereas turning it into a function template would generate multiple copies (which could needlessly increase executable size, depending on various factors).</p>
<hr />
<p>If there are a lot of call sites that need extra parameters, it might be convenient to overload <code>BigFunction</code> with a function template that wraps the lambda creation. The compiler will generate multiple copies of this wrapper, but there still would be only one copy of the big function (and its calculations) in the executable.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class... Args&gt;
double BigFunction(double (*func)(double, Args...), double var, Args... params) {
    return BigFunction([=](double var2) { return func(var2, params...); }, var);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This would give you the <code>BigFunction(func, var, para)</code> calling syntax that matches your second case.</p>
<p>I'd be inclined to leave the lambda definition to the callers for maximum flexibility, but this wrapper is an option.</p>
